steps.detail:
Anaphylaxis can start within minutes of contact with something the person is allergic to. Common triggers are foods (such as nuts or milk), insect stings, medicines and latex.

Signs include:
- swelling of the face, neck, tongue or lips;
- trouble breathing, wheezing, or a feeling the throat is closing;
- skin rash or hives (hives are common but can be absent in severe cases);
- signs of shock: pale, cool or clammy skin, fast weak pulse, confusion, fainting;
- becoming drowsy or unresponsive.

A history of allergy, or known contact with their allergen, makes it more likely.

Do not wait for collapse or low blood pressure before giving epinephrine. Giving it early can stop shock developing. Delays of more than about 20 minutes are linked to more deaths.
